$DIR/main.p:32:8: cannot pass "wrapped: %w" to fmt.Errorf, which keeps it after returning
    "wrapped: %w" points into ev. ev is a local variable of f (declared at
    main.p:30). fmt.Errorf stores a pointer loaded from args[i] in memory that
    outlives the call (errorf.p:45), so "wrapped: %w" would outlive ev.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If fmt.Errorf should keep its own copy of ev, change it to take the value,
    not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[E] from new, a shared[E],
    or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
